<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.6.4: Recognizing Related Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00157</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.6.4: Recognizing Related Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e78f4665-a498-4b7b-9760-5512236d2895</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para>A cardboard box, which weighs 0.6 pound when empty, is filled with 15 bags of beans and
        a 4-pound bag of rice. The total weight of the box and the contents inside it is 25.6
        pounds. One way to represent this situation is with the equation \( 0.6 + 15b + 4 = 25.6 \).</para>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>In this situation, what does the solution to the equation represent?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>The solution is the weight of a bag of beans.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

        <exercise id="e95f1302-e095-488f-b28c-277cdb16ef09" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Identify the <emphasis>three</emphasis> equations that are equivalent to
              0.6+15b+4=25.6.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The correct answers are \(15b+4=25\), \(3(0.6+15b+4)=76.8\), \(15b=21\).
              Correctly applying the properties of real numbers and using inverse operations helps
              you recognize equivalent equations.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answers are \(15b+4=25\), \(3(0.6+15b+4)=76.8\), \(15b=21\).
              \(15b=21\). Correctly applying the properties of real numbers and using inverse
              operations helps you recognize equivalent equations.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
